# shellcheck shell=sh
#
# WHICH PATHS MOVE THE GENERATED MAPS — ONE OWNER, TWO CONSUMERS.
#
# Sourced by `.githooks/atlas-stage` (the pre-commit regeneration, card #501)
# and by `.githooks/pre-push` (the branch-push freshness gate, cards #606 and
# #519). It is a file rather than a copy in each because a second list shadowing
# a source of truth always drifts from it (working law 4) — and the drift here
# would be invisible: a hook that stopped firing on a root the generator scans
# looks exactly like a hook with nothing to do.
#
# ⚠ TWO FILTERS, AND THEIR WIDTHS ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT.
#
# MATCHES decides whether to REGENERATE, and over-firing costs nine seconds, so
# it is wide (all of `client/`, `scripts/`, every extension).
#
# WARN_MATCHES decides whether to tell the author their map may not match the
# commit — and, at push time, whether a staleness verdict can be trusted at all.
# Over-firing costs the warning's credibility, which is worth more than nine
# seconds. So it is the population the generators actually READ, in three parts:
# the architecture generator's own `SCANNED_ROOTS` **narrowed to its
# `SOURCE_EXTENSIONS`**, the extra inputs of `fingerprint()`
# (`annotations.yaml`), and the CAPABILITY generator's two module inputs, which
# live under `scripts/`.
#
# **Both narrowings were measured rather than reasoned.** The first version
# warned on MATCHES, and the very first commit it ran on warned about an
# untracked `scripts/_*-disposable.mts` while `pnpm architecture:check` said the
# map was fresh — every shift carries untracked disposables there, so it would
# have fired on nearly every commit the team makes. The second review of PR #517
# then found the mirror defect: warning on ANY file under a scanned root fires
# on unstaged `drizzle/*.sql` that `fingerprint()` never hashes, and warning on
# no file under `scripts/` is SILENT when the capability corpus is dirty — which
# is a clean "map of its own tree" message over a `capability:check` that reds.
#
# ⚠ ONE KNOWN GAP, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED: `git ls-files --others
# --exclude-standard` hides gitignored files, while the generator's `walk()`
# reads the disk regardless of gitignore. A gitignored `.ts` under a scanned
# root therefore moves the map without warning. Rare, and not worth listing
# ignored files on every commit.
#
# Held to the generators by `server/atlasCommitHook.test.ts`: WARN_MATCHES must
# fire on every `SCANNED_ROOTS` entry at every `SOURCE_EXTENSIONS` extension, on
# every extra input of `fingerprint()`, and on every relative import of
# `generate-capability-atlas.mts`; and MATCHES must be a superset of it. The
# capability arm resolves ONE hop, so a new input reached only through
# `lib/capabilityAtlas.mts` is not seen — which is why that arm says so.
MATCHES='^(server/|client/|shared/|scripts/|drizzle/|docs/architecture/annotations\.yaml$)'
WARN_MATCHES='^((server|client/src|shared|drizzle)/.*\.(ts|tsx|css|mts)|docs/architecture/annotations\.yaml|scripts/(capability-atlas-corpus|lib/capabilityAtlas)\.mts)$'
